\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\title{Reference pairings, adjoints and parity}
\date{2026-10-20}

\p{To compare a field operator's input and output through a bilinear
form, a reference pairing represents its adjoint. This is the return
operation used later for incidence and weighted energy.
The [finite field module](fdip-0010) has a perfect counting pairing
over any commutative unital ring. Its represented adjoint is an
ordinary linear product-reversing involution; neither positivity nor
division by two is required.}

\p{A partition of the carrier splits fields by restriction and extension
by zero. It also separates operators that preserve the two parts from
those that exchange them, giving a [parity grading](fdip-001K).
The [four-cycle calculation](fdip-001M) exhibits an odd invertible shift
and a difference with both parity components. This distinguishes parity
from the extra requirement of a square-zero differential, before
[paired differences](fdip-0016) and
[elimination of a chosen summand](fdip-0020).}

